WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Spain builds a kilometer of high-speed rail for 1-9th the price Britain pays for HS2. This

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.560
is First Principles Daily, episode 162. Earthworks, concrete, steel rail, overhead electrification,

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.260
and signaling all trade at comparable world prices. Researchers tracking dozens of projects

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.640
worldwide trace most of the spread in delivered totals to how each country organizes the work,

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.440
not to differences in the ground. The split appears before any track is laid. In the sequence

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.660
of studies, approvals, and contract interfaces that must be finished first.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.140
High-speed rail is assembled from civil engineering steps that have been executed for decades.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.860
In most countries, the process begins with root selection, environmental reviews, land

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:49.100
acquisition, and a sequence of design reviews involving multiple agencies.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.220
Each review layer adds studies, consultants, and revised drawings.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.900
Procurement then splits the work among separate contractors for earthworks, structures, track,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:04.160
power, and signaling, and each contract carries its own contingencies and interfaces.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.100
In the United Kingdom, these steps have multiplied.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:13.780
The Financial Times analysis of HS2 data shows that contractor margins, environmental mitigations,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.080
and successive planning approvals together account for the largest share of the recorded

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:18.540
premium.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.160
Spain kept the same sequence inside a single public body, Adif, which maintained its own engineering

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:29.420
staff and issued standard drawings for viaducts, tunnels, and track forms.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.800
The Transit Costs project final report notes that countries maintaining in-house capacity

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.060
and a small number of standard designs avoid the repeated redesign cycles that inflate soft

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.000
costs elsewhere.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.040
Every added study or interface meeting carries salaries, office overhead, and scheduled delay,

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.280
so the cumulative effect appears in the final bill even when the concrete and steel quantities

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.180
remain similar.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:58.920
One objection sometimes raised is that Britain faces denser population and stricter environmental

00:01:58.920 --> 00:01:59.420
rules.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.380
The same sources show that Spain completed lines through comparable terrain and protected

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.080
areas, while keeping the number of separate sign-off bodies low.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.740
The difference therefore lies less in the rules themselves than in whether those rules

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.580
are administered through one technical agency, or through successive layers of consultants

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.740
and local authorities.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:24.460
When each layer must produce its own risk register and contingency allowance, the allowances

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.340
compound before construction begins.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.200
The calendar from authorization to first poor grows longer, which raises financing costs

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.700
and exposes the project to inflation in materials and labor, even though the physical quantities

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.700
per kilometer stay roughly constant.

00:02:40.613 --> 00:02:45.073
The magic wand number is what a thing would cost if you could arrange the raw atoms into

00:02:45.073 --> 00:02:48.453
the finished object for free and pay only for the materials.

00:02:48.933 --> 00:02:50.513
That is the theoretical floor.

00:02:51.073 --> 00:02:54.633
The first principle's ratio is the finished price divided by that floor.

00:02:55.213 --> 00:02:58.073
Near one, the thing is already close to the physical limit.

00:02:58.653 --> 00:03:04.393
A large ratio means most of the price is design, process, organization, or rules, which

00:03:04.393 --> 00:03:06.973
is where a from scratch rethink has room to work.

00:03:07.513 --> 00:03:09.413
The ratio says where the price sits.

00:03:09.913 --> 00:03:12.153
It is not a verdict on the people who built the thing.

00:03:12.153 --> 00:03:16.373
The sources supplied for this episode do not contain the commodity prices.

00:03:16.953 --> 00:03:21.593
Material quantities or labor-hour estimates needed to construct that floor for a kilometer

00:03:21.593 --> 00:03:22.693
of high-speed line.

00:03:23.273 --> 00:03:27.453
Without those inputs, no first principles ratio can be calculated from the record.

00:03:28.093 --> 00:03:33.233
What the same sources do document is the size of the observed cost gap and the institutional

00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:34.413
steps that produce it.

00:03:34.993 --> 00:03:39.833
The Inneco Analysis of the Transit Costs Project Database places Spain's weighted average

00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:46.793
at 17.7 million euros per kilometer in 2022 prices, while the corresponding figure for the rest

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:55.193
of the sample sits at 45.5 million euros. Divide 45.5 by 17.7 and the wider sample lands a bit

00:03:55.193 --> 00:04:00.633
above two and a half times the Spanish figure. For a kilometer whose earth, concrete, and steel

00:04:00.633 --> 00:04:06.393
are bought in the same world market. The Transit Costs project attributes the bulk of these premiums

00:04:06.393 --> 00:04:11.793
to three recurring factors, the number of separate agencies that must sign off, the absence

00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:16.973
of reusable standard designs, and the reliance on external consultants rather than retained

00:04:16.973 --> 00:04:18.293
public sector engineers.

00:04:18.973 --> 00:04:23.953
Each factor lengthens the calendar between authorization and construction start, raising

00:04:23.953 --> 00:04:28.173
financing and escalation costs that compound before any concrete is poured.

00:04:28.693 --> 00:04:33.573
Because the physical inputs themselves are internationally traded, the documented divergent

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:38.693
sits almost entirely in these organizational layers rather than in the price of steel or cement.

00:04:39.373 --> 00:04:43.813
Differences in labor rates or ground conditions are an obvious place to look for the spread.

00:04:44.493 --> 00:04:48.913
The data show, however, that the largest premiums appear in soft-cost categories,

00:04:49.413 --> 00:04:55.013
planning, legal, and management overhead, rather than in the direct cost of earthworks or structures.

00:04:55.713 --> 00:04:59.793
When the same earth-moving equipment and concrete mixes are available worldwide,

00:04:59.793 --> 00:05:07.273
The price gap traces to how many times the design is redrawn, and how many separate profit margins are stacked on top of one another.

00:05:08.291 --> 00:05:13.011
Spain's Ministry of Transport and Adif have already demonstrated one workable path.

00:05:13.631 --> 00:05:18.931
They retained a core engineering group that produced a family of standard viaduct and tunnel sections,

00:05:19.411 --> 00:05:22.691
reduced the number of external approvals required for each segment,

00:05:23.191 --> 00:05:28.511
and let the same teams move from one line to the next without relearning the specification each time.

00:05:28.511 --> 00:05:33.911
The INICO report records that this continuity kept average costs inside a narrow

00:05:33.911 --> 00:05:39.051
band across 10 projects whose lengths ranged from under 100 kilometers to more

00:05:39.051 --> 00:05:43.691
than 400 kilometers. A comparable redesign elsewhere would begin by

00:05:43.691 --> 00:05:48.491
consolidating approval authority into a single technical agency, publishing and

00:05:48.491 --> 00:05:52.811
freezing a set of standard drawings, and rebuilding in-house estimating and

00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:57.151
supervision capacity so that consultants are used only for specialized tasks.

00:05:57.151 --> 00:06:02.191
Those three moves would shrink the largest measured premiums identified in the transit

00:06:02.191 --> 00:06:03.711
cost project case studies.

00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:06.471
The remaining hard constraints are political.

00:06:07.191 --> 00:06:12.551
Land acquisition still requires public consent, environmental rules still impose real limits,

00:06:13.011 --> 00:06:16.231
and any new agency must still answer to elected officials.

00:06:16.991 --> 00:06:21.871
None of those constraints disappears, yet the Spanish record shows they can be met at far

00:06:21.871 --> 00:06:24.911
lower cost once the organizational overhead is trimmed.

00:06:24.911 --> 00:06:35.331
The first projects built under such a regime would still face site-specific adjustments, but the volume of unique engineering would drop sharply once the baseline sections are frozen.

00:06:35.991 --> 00:06:42.331
Standard designs lose their advantage the moment every project is forced to renegotiate them with a fresh set of agencies.

00:06:43.011 --> 00:06:53.851
The advantage was never a clever or viaduct on the page. It was permission to reuse a section already accepted, so the next segment does not hire another round of consultants to redraw it.

00:06:53.851 --> 00:06:59.171
does not open another contract interface and does not stack another margin on a cross-section

00:06:59.171 --> 00:07:00.791
the last line already built.

00:07:01.391 --> 00:07:05.971
Force that re-negotiation and the standard becomes the starting point of the same soft-cost

00:07:05.971 --> 00:07:12.571
stack, planning, legal work, management overhead, a fresh risk register, a fresh contingency.

00:07:13.031 --> 00:07:18.351
Those allowances compound before the first pour and the tons of concrete per kilometer barely

00:07:18.351 --> 00:07:20.011
have to change for the bill to move.

00:07:20.011 --> 00:07:25.551
Retaining a permanent engineering staff inside the delivery body is what keeps the reuse real.

00:07:26.051 --> 00:07:30.211
The staff's accumulated drawings turn into an asset the agency already holds.

00:07:30.651 --> 00:07:36.531
Rather than an expense paid again each time an outside team is assembled to learn the specification from the beginning.

00:07:37.111 --> 00:07:40.851
A consultant brought in for a specialized task can still be the right call.

00:07:41.291 --> 00:07:45.771
A consultant brought in to rediscover a track form the public engineers already hold

00:07:45.771 --> 00:07:49.071
is the repeated expense that arrangement exists to stop.

00:07:49.071 --> 00:07:53.851
The drawings only cheap in the next kilometer if the people who know them are still inside

00:07:53.851 --> 00:07:55.431
the body that lets the contracts.

00:07:55.951 --> 00:08:00.611
A further step is to publish those standard drawings as binding requirements rather than

00:08:00.611 --> 00:08:05.391
as optional guidance, so that each new line does not reopen the same design debates.

00:08:06.151 --> 00:08:11.571
Optional guidance leaves every ordinary viaduct, tunnel and track form free to be argued again,

00:08:12.131 --> 00:08:15.611
and an argument that must be had again is where the layers grow back.

00:08:15.611 --> 00:08:20.471
A requirement closes that argument and leaves the constraints that were always real standing

00:08:20.471 --> 00:08:21.191
where they belong.

00:08:21.751 --> 00:08:27.531
Consent for the land, the limits in the environmental rules, and an agency that still answers to elected

00:08:27.531 --> 00:08:28.011
officials.

00:08:28.571 --> 00:08:33.091
The first concrete signal that another country is attempting the same shift would be the publication

00:08:33.091 --> 00:08:38.591
of a single binding technical manual that subsequent lines are required to follow without new

00:08:38.591 --> 00:08:40.551
environmental or planning overlays.

00:08:40.551 --> 00:08:45.451
Will that manual appear and will the next authorized line actually be bound by it?

00:08:45.871 --> 00:08:47.731
That's First Principles Daily for Today.

00:08:48.291 --> 00:08:52.411
If this changed how you see what things cost, share it with someone who'd appreciate it

00:08:52.411 --> 00:08:53.991
and subscribe wherever you listen.

00:08:54.571 --> 00:08:59.991
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, one example or one opportunity, every day, see you tomorrow.

